---
title: "Cards on hover"
---

A long document is full of references that point elsewhere: "see @fig-flow", "as
@sec-why explains", a footnote[^aside]. Following one means jumping away and losing your
place. Hovering a reference here pops a small card that renders the *target itself*, drawn
straight from the page by its content-hash id, so you can glance without leaving.

## Why it works {#sec-why}

Every block carries a stable id, so the card shows the actual target, not a guessed
snippet. A heading shows itself plus its first lines; a figure shows the figure; a
footnote shows the note.

See @sec-why above, or the diagram in @fig-flow below.

```{mermaid}
%%| label: fig-flow
%%| fig-cap: "Hover a reference to preview its target."
flowchart LR
  Reference -->|hover| Card
  Card -->|renders| Target
```

The card is read-only and reuses what the renderer already computed, so it costs nothing
extra at build time.

[^aside]: This footnote is itself a hover target: hovering its reference shows this text.
